# agent-harness:project-gitignore:v1:start
# Managed block. Its source is .agents/templates/gitignore in the harness repository; edit it
# there and re-run EnsureProject. Anything you add outside these markers is preserved.

# Task-hook runtime state. hook-state.js writes <repo-root>/taskstate/<project-id>/ by its own
# design -- session-local records, allow-tag usage among them -- which belong to one device and
# one session rather than to the project. It was never ignored, so every project running the
# hooks accumulated an untracked directory in its root that a `git add -A` would commit.
taskstate/

# The pre-relocation location for the same state. A session that predates the move still writes
# here, so a project can acquire this directory without any current tool creating it.
.claude/state/

# The codex dispatch marker. auto-schedule-codex-poll.js writes it into <repo-root>/.claude/ on
# PostToolUse for a codex subagent, and its own body tells the session to delete it once the
# wakeup is scheduled -- so a session that ends before that leaves it behind. Named rather than
# ignoring `.claude/`, which is a real product configuration directory a project may track.
.claude/codex-dispatch-pending.json

# Linked worktrees kept inside the repository. A worktree's own files are tracked by ITS checkout,
# never by this one, so an unignored .worktrees/ shows as untracked here and a `git add -A` would
# commit a second copy of the tree. The convention is one place per repository: agent worktrees go
# in .worktrees/<task>, product-managed ones stay wherever their product puts them. On 2026-08-14
# nine agent-harness worktrees were spread across four roots, two of them siblings of the project
# in Projects\, where they read as separate projects.
.worktrees/

# Work Scope transaction scratch. State and evidence writes go to a temp file beside the target and
# are renamed into place, so a write killed mid-transaction -- a hook timeout, an interrupted
# session, a closed laptop -- leaves the temp behind. Nothing sweeps them: `Reconcile-WorkState
# -Repair` reports the state valid and drift-free while the leftovers stay on disk. Measured
# 2026-08-15 in this repository: six of them untracked, which a `git add -A` would have committed.
.agents/work/tmp/
.agents/work/**/*.tmp

# Delegate progress chatter is ephemeral and must not become committed branch noise.
.agents/work/statusboard/

# The lane-hold marker the router writes into every worktree it creates
# (Invoke-FleetRoute.ps1). The router already skips it as work evidence, but it was not ignored, so
# the reap guard saw one uncommitted file and refused the pre-authorized reap of every lane
# worktree -- which is how 48 of them accumulated until the device ran out of disk.
.agents-hold

# Intake entries are JSON filings, never working trees. Block both the known checkout directory
# and Git administrative markers at any depth below imports.
.agents/work/imports/**/filing-worktree/
.agents/work/imports/**/.git
.agents/work/imports/**/.git/

# Compiled-assembly cache for WorkScope.psm1's two C# blocks. The module used to re-run the C#
# compiler on every import -- 742 ms of a ~900 ms import, measured 2026-08-15 under pwsh 7.6.4,
# paid by every process the task hooks start. It now compiles once to a DLL beside itself, keyed
# by a hash of the source and the runtime version. Build output, never source: it is rebuilt on
# demand, differs per machine and per pwsh version, and a committed copy would be loaded as code
# on someone else's machine.
.agents/tools/.assembly-cache/

# Work Scope's own authoritative runtime files -- the hash-chained event log, the state it
# describes, its schema, and the evidence and handoff directories a closed task's receipts live
# in. A project's OWN earlier or broader pattern (a catch-all `*.jsonl`, `*.json`, or similar) can
# still swallow these from every commit with nothing reporting it: measured on mission-control,
# whose `*.jsonl` rule excluded .agents/work/events.jsonl from history entirely, so the chain that
# authenticates state.json was never committed. THIS NEGATION IS NOT A RELIABLE FIX BY ITSELF: git
# applies the LAST matching .gitignore line, so a project rule that appears anywhere BELOW this
# managed block (the common case -- Set-MarkedBlock keeps or writes this block near the top) still
# wins over the negation below and re-excludes the path. Confirmed by hand: placing this exact
# negation at the end of this block did not stop `git check-ignore -v` from reporting
# mission-control's later `*.jsonl` rule as the winner. Keep this as defense-in-depth for the
# projects where no later rule conflicts, but Test-WorkScopeSchemaProjection.ps1's gitignore-exposure
# check is the safeguard that actually catches the swallowed case, by running real `git
# check-ignore` per enrolled project rather than trusting pattern order in the abstract.
#
# On 2026-09-02 events.jsonl reached 130 MB in committed history on the harness repository, over
# GitHub's 100 MB hard limit, blocking all pushes of any branch containing those commits.
# The event log cannot be tracked at its current size. state.json is its derived, mutable pointer;
# tracking that half alone moves it across generations on merges and checkout switches while the
# local log does not move. Keep the pair local rather than manufacturing unreplayable divergence.
.agents/work/events.jsonl
.agents/work/event-archives/*.jsonl
.agents/work/state.json
# The Work Scope lock beside it is the same machine-local state: a tracked copy rides into every
# clone and Reconcile-WorkState reports it as a stale lock with no live owner.
.agents/work/state.lock
# Splice-WorkStateEventChains.ps1 writes a per-splice receipt (renumbered event range, source
# hashes, merge point) beside the event log it merges; OutputPath must stay under .agents/work
# so the receipt is device-local runtime evidence like events.jsonl, never tracked history.
.agents/work/splice-receipts/
# WS-10/WS-11 (Ws.psm1): lane events and receipts, the inbox, and the interim per-track task files
# are device-local runtime state like state.json; WS-13 moves the plan to the workscope ref.
.agents/work/inbox.jsonl
.agents/work/lanes/
.agents/work/tracks/
# Work Scope archive lines (WS-4, Invoke-WorkScopeArchive.ps1) are tier-2 plan data that belongs
# on the workscope orphan ref (D15, D17), never on master; machine-local like state.json until
# ws sync carries them.
.agents/work/archive/
# The fleet dispatch registry is the same kind of thing as state.json: live, mutable,
# machine-local runtime state. It records the PID, start time and target directory of every
# dispatch this machine made, so it is meaningless on any other machine and it changes on
# every dispatch and every completion -- rewritten by Invoke-FleetRoute.ps1 on every lane open
# and close, with a sibling lock file. Tracking it would dirty every checkout and would put one
# machine's in-flight lanes and process table into every other machine's history on each pull.
.agents/work/fleet-registry.json
.agents/work/fleet-registry.json.lock
.agents/work/fleet-registry.json.tmp-*
# The backburner sidecar PR 589 split out of state.json. Same class as state.json above:
# local machine state, 2.3 MB of queue, and tracking it would commit one machine's queue over
# every other machine's on each pull.
.agents/work/backburner.json
# Per-worktree generated task views. A linked worktree reads Work Scope state from the main
# checkout but inherits the ROOT TASK.md frozen at the commit it was created from, so lanes were
# briefed on work finished days earlier. The views are rendered here instead -- and they must stay
# untracked for the same reason state.json does: a tracked per-worktree TASK.md rides into that
# lane's pull request and conflicts with every other lane's copy on landing.
.agents/work/TASK.md
.agents/work/BACKBURNER.md
.agents/work/LOG.md
.agents/work/PROJECT.md
.agents/work/TRACKS.md
!.agents/work/schema.json
!.agents/work/evidence/
!.agents/handoffs/
.impeccable/

# The Codex CLI writes error.log into whatever working directory it is invoked from, so it lands
# in the root of any repository an agent works in. It is a debug trace, not project content, and
# it carries the signed-in ChatGPT account id on every startup line -- an identifier that must
# never be committed. Measured 2026-09-16: an enrolled project had a 2956-line error.log sitting
# untracked with no ignore rule covering it, one `git add -A` from being published. The harness
# repository itself already ignores it; every project the harness enrols was missing the rule,
# because this template never carried it.
error.log
# Harness lane scratch is generated runtime debris. Session imports, hook-misfire records, and
# route briefs are written into each enrolled project worktree and must not make a finished lane
# unreapable. Keep this scoped to the scratch producers so authored Work Scope state remains
# governed by its own explicit rules.
.agents/work/imports/
.agents/work/misfires/
.agents/fleet-briefs/
# agent-harness:project-gitignore:v1:end

node_modules/
dist/
.DS_Store
